        "  message: false\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "**Researcher:** Dasey Dang\n",
        "**Date:** July 29, 2026\n",
        "\n",
        "**Research Or Analytical Question:**\n",
        "Which optimizer produces the strongest validation performance for a three-class neural network predicting transportation-use dominance from participants' open-ended descriptions of their ideal transportation?\n",
        "\n",
        "## Answer, Response, + Summary of Results\n",
        "\n",
        "Adam is the most effective of the three optimizers tested for this neural-network architecture and data split.\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the transportation modeling data, I compared Adam, RMSprop, and stochastic gradient descent (SGD) while holding the neural-network architecture constant. The outcome included three classes: public transit dominant, rideshare dominant, and no dominant mode. The data were divided using a stratified 70/15/15 train, validation, and test split with a fixed seed of 1738.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because the outcome classes were imbalanced, macro-F1 was used as the primary validation metric. Adam produced the strongest validation performance with a macro-F1 of **0.4270** and validation accuracy of **0.6250**. RMSprop and SGD each produced a macro-F1 of **0.2342** and validation accuracy of **0.5417**.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](optimizer_comparison.png){fig-alt=\"Bar chart comparing validation macro-F1 across Adam, RMSprop, and SGD.\" width=70%}\n",
        "\n",
        "## What questions or uncertainties remain? (Need not be exhaustive)\n",
        "\n",
        "Would class weighting improve the model's ability to classify rideshare-dominant cases?\n",
        "\n",
        "Would Adam remain the strongest optimizer if learning rates were separately tuned for Adam, RMSprop, and SGD?\n",
        "\n",
        "How stable are these optimizer results across different reproducible train, validation, and test splits?\n",
        "\n",
        "Would the relative performance of the optimizers change with a larger sample, particularly with more rideshare-dominant observations?"
      ],
      "id": "6243f0e5"
    }
  ],
